//? Title: Stacked Autoencoders: Greedy Fits, Exact Transfer and Joint Fine-Tuning
//? Subtitle: Frozen representations, both learned decoders, eligible epoch-zero baselines and a scratch control
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A deep autoencoder can be initialized by training shallow reconstruction models in sequence.
This report first fits a 784–64–784 sigmoid model on MNIST, freezes its selected encoder and
caches its outputs, then fits a 64–16–64 model on those actual representations. Both encoders
and both decoders transfer exactly into a 784–64–16–64–784 stack before joint fine-tuning.
A separate scratch stack has the same full architecture and joint-phase batch order. The report
keeps every Adam update and validation choice explicit, derives the decoder composition and
an error bound, and checks restoration, frozen caches and code controls. Fine-tuned test MSE is
0.06606, versus 0.06818 for scratch and 0.06780 for the fitting mean. The staged path uses nine
data passes against scratch's three, so this short comparison cannot isolate a causal benefit
of pretraining or establish a compute-matched advantage.
)
\toc

> Two shallow objectives, then one full objective

Let \(f_1:X\mapsto Y\) and \(g_1:Y\mapsto\hat X\) be the first sigmoid encoder/decoder;
let \(f_2:Y\mapsto Z\) and \(g_2:Z\mapsto\hat Y\) be the second. The experiment solves
three different finite-step fitting problems:

$$
\min_{f_1,g_1}\operatorname{MSE}(g_1(f_1(X)),X),\qquad
Y=\operatorname{stopgrad}(f_1(X)),\qquad
\min_{f_2,g_2}\operatorname{MSE}(g_2(f_2(Y)),Y),\\
\text{then jointly optimize }\quad
\operatorname{MSE}\bigl(g_1(g_2(f_2(f_1(X)))),X\bigr).
$$
\alttext(The first shallow autoencoder learns pixels, the second learns frozen first-encoder representations, and joint fine-tuning then scores the composed four-layer stack against original pixels.)

[Bengio, Lamblin, Popovici and Larochelle](https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2006/hash/5da713a690c067105aeb2fae32403405-Abstract.html)
study greedy training followed by whole-network refinement. This report retains the source's
small all-sigmoid MSE experiment, not a reproduction of its published classification results.
There are no labels in any loss, tied decoder weights, RBM steps or hidden fitting constructors.
Stage two sees cached representations from the **selected frozen** first model, not random
features, raw images or a concurrently changing encoder.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/stacked_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(54, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "MNIST pixel rows\n784 coordinates")
      .node("s1", {6, 0}, "Stage 1: 784 → 64 → 784\nSelect by validation, then freeze")
      .node("y", {12, 0}, "Cache selected f1(X)\n64 detached target coordinates")
      .node("s2", {12, -3}, "Stage 2: 64 → 16 → 64\nLearn the cached targets")
      .node("t", {6, -6}, "Transfer all eight arrays\nBoth encoders and both decoders")
      .node("f", {0, -6}, "Fine-tune the full stack\nOriginal pixels are targets")
      .edge("x", "s1")
      .edge("s1", "y")
      .edge("y", "s2")
      .edge("s2", "t")
      .edge("t", "f")
      .edge("x", "f")
      .save_svg("build/stacked_method.svg");
}
